          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottfried+Leibniz"} Gottfried Leibniz
            said (in translation from
            %a{:href => "#Lewis18"} Lewis 1918, p. 373
            with original Latin terms in parenthesis; see also
            %a{:href => "#Cartwright71"} Cartwright 1971, p. 119
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#GriesSchneider98"} Gries &amp; Schneider 1998
          %blockquote
            %p
              Two terms are the same (eadem) if one can be substituted for the otherwithout altering the truth of any statement (salva veritate). If we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              enters into some true proposition, and the substitution of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              wherever it appears results in a new proposition that is likewise true, and if this can be done for every proposition, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              are said to be the same; conversely, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
              are the same, they can be substituted for one another.
          %p
            The converse mentioned at the end of the last sentence is known as the
            %strong principle of substitution
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong indiscernibility of identicals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi T
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\forall_{x,y\colon T} (x =_T y) \implies (P(x) \iff P(y))
          %p
            In the interpretation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions+as+types"} propositions as types
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            are interpreted as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
            becomes a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+type"} dependent product type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            becomes a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+family"} type family
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/implication"} implication
            becomes a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+equivalence"} logical equivalence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            becomes an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+type"} equivalence type
            %em transport
            in type theory, which for the type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and the type family
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
